nodecode-penny is a rewrite of HelperLabs's HelperLabs/penny-hermes:

  https://github.com/HelperLabs/penny-hermes
  commit 0887dae11062165a78be9fb6c7b85c810a3353b5
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Helper Labs (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes memory provider: it registered with the Hermes
runtime, which put a block of Penny's core memory and recently recalled notes
into the system prompt of every turn, fetched in a background thread from
Penny's hosted MCP server, and offered three tools -- penny_profile,
penny_search and penny_remember -- over that server's penny_read and
penny_write. Its OAuth login (dynamic client registration, PKCE, a loopback
callback, tokens kept in the Hermes profile) was a subcommand of its own.
This rewrite keeps the three tools, their arguments, the bounds they
enforced, the endpoint (https://app.mypenny.ai/mcp), MCP revision
2025-03-26, the exchange the plugin's client used (initialize,
notifications/initialized, tools/call) and the rule that a save counts only
when Penny answers with a receipt; it speaks that endpoint through nlk:http,
so it needs no MCP transport and no Hermes runtime. The token is read from
the cell's own section -- inline, from a named variable, or from a named
file, a Penny CLI's auth.json included -- because a cell has no subcommand
of its own to run a login. Left out: the automatic prompt prefetch (a cell's
model calls penny_profile and penny_search when it needs them), the plugin's
OAuth login flow with its browser callback, its private-only scoping over
spaces (the cell renders the scope Penny labels each note with), and its
refusal to run outside a primary local conversation, which the cell's tools
have no equivalent of; a saved note names its source as "nodecode" rather
than "hermes". Nothing of the original is vendored.
